PowerShell Script: Disable Ctrl+Alt+Del Requirement
# Disable Ctrl+Alt+Del requirement for logon
$RegPath = "HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System"

# Create the key if it doesn't exist
if (-not (Test-Path $RegPath)) {
    New-Item -Path $RegPath -Force | Out-Null
}

# Set the value for disabling Ctrl+Alt+Del
New-ItemProperty `
    -Path $RegPath `
    -Name "DisableCAD" `
    -PropertyType DWORD `
    -Value 1 `
    -Force | Out-Null

Write-Output "Ctrl+Alt+Del requirement has been disabled."

🔄 Force Group Policy Update (Optional)

If you'd like the changes to take effect immediately (without rebooting), run:

gpupdate /force

❌ Revert to Default (Enable Ctrl+Alt+Del Requirement)

If you ever want to re-enable the Ctrl+Alt+Del requirement, you can run the following:

Remove-ItemProperty `
    -Path "HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System" `
    -Name "DisableCAD" `
    -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue

Write-Output "Ctrl+Alt+Del requirement has been re-enabled."

⚠️ Notes

This setting affects all users on the machine (since it's under HKLM).

Security Considerations: Disabling Ctrl+Alt+Del reduces a security feature that helps prevent malicious software from hijacking the logon screen. Ensure that your environment has adequate other security measures if you choose to disable this.
